# Target C — audited §170 fully correlated sparse-return exclusion through `sigma=0.0172`

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only full-core route theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module normalizes the theorem and hostile audit in
`docs/reports/target_c_sparse_split_fully_correlated_window_0172_2026-09-12.md` and
`docs/audits/target_c_sparse_split_fully_correlated_window_0172_hostile_audit_2026-09-12.md`.

The earlier sparse-return proof bounded its directional product and sparse penalty
separately, even though both spend the same slack. Keeping the full shared simplex
correlated gives the stronger exclusion

\[
\boxed{0\le\sigma\le s_0:=0.0172.}
\tag{170.1}
\]

The exact scalar envelope of the same argument first vanishes only near

\[
\sigma_{env}=0.0173116294\ldots,
\]

but no theorem endpoint at that root is claimed. The safe value `0.0172` retains a
normalized contradiction margin above `0.00232`.

Here `sigma` is the Section-118 sparse-split slack, not the train atomic mass denoted
`sigma` in §§164/168.

---

## 170.1 Correlated starting inequality

Retain the sparse/directional decomposition

\[
x+y+z+w=\sigma,
\qquad x,y,z,w\ge0,
\tag{170.2}
\]

and the necessary signed-return condition

\[
\lambda z\ge\kappa[J]_+,
\qquad
\kappa:=2-\sqrt3,
\tag{170.3}
\]

where

\[
\begin{aligned}
J={}&A_*(x,w)
-\lambda\left(10z+{x\over2}+{25w\over3}\right)\\
&-2\left(U-x-y+2z\right)
\left(Q(y)+y+x+{10w\over3}\right),
\end{aligned}
\tag{170.4}
\]

\[
A_*(x,w)
={\pi\over4}-G(\theta)-2G(\phi_8+r)-G(r),
\qquad
r=x+{10w\over3},
\tag{170.5}
\]

\[
G(t)={t-\sin t\over8},
\]

and `Q(y)` is the regular-tooth inverse determined by

\[
\sin Q+\sqrt3(1-\cos Q)-Q={y\over2}.
\tag{170.6}
\]

Define

\[
\widetilde J:=J-{\lambda z\over\kappa}.
\tag{170.7}
\]

If `widetilde J>0`, then (170.3) gives

\[
\lambda z\ge\kappa J>\lambda z,
\]

impossible. It therefore suffices to prove a uniform positive lower bound for
`widetilde J`.

Let `theta_0` solve

\[
3\theta_0-2\pi-\sin(\theta_0/2)-2\sin\theta_0=s_0.
\]

At `s_0=0.0172`,

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\theta_0&=2.706564629265182\ldots,\\
U_0&=1.836508580615961\ldots,\\
\lambda_0&=0.976436949372909\ldots,\\
\phi_0&=0.870056048649222\ldots .
\end{aligned}}
\tag{170.8}
\]

For every `sigma<=s_0`, monotonicity gives

\[
\theta\le\theta_0,
\qquad
U\le U_0,
\qquad
\lambda\le\lambda_0.
\tag{170.9}
\]

Since `G` is increasing, replacing `theta,U,lambda` by the endpoint values lowers
`widetilde J` and is conservative.

Enlarge the physical equality simplex to

\[
x+y+z+w\le s_0.
\tag{170.10}
\]

For fixed `x,y,z`, the relaxed lower bound is strictly decreasing in `w`; the area,
sparse penalty, and directional-product derivatives are all negative in that variable.
Hence every relaxed minimizer lies on

\[
\boxed{x+y+z+w=s_0.}
\tag{170.11}
\]

Filling unused slack with `w` is only an optimization device; no fictitious physical
source is asserted.

---

## 170.2 Geometry remains in the audited branch

The hostile-audited largest-gap transfer gives derivative above `0.30` for
`theta>=theta_8`, `0<=delta<=0.1`, while the coarse `delta>=0.1` branch costs more than
`0.0326`. Since `w<=0.0172`, the coarse branch remains impossible and

\[
\boxed{\delta\le {10\over3}w.}
\tag{170.12}
\]

The exact correlated identity then gives

\[
x_2+x_3+\tau
=\delta+x
\le x+{10\over3}w
\le {10\over3}s_0
=0.0573333\ldots .
\tag{170.13}
\]

This is far below the residual principal gap `phi_0>0.8700`, so the fourth-gap and
adjacent-span branch stays strict.

The oriented support extent satisfies

\[
U_0+{10s_0\over3}
=1.8938419\ldots<\pi,
\tag{170.14}
\]

so the tangent lift remains single-valued. The coarser corridor-mismatch margin remains
positive (`>0.0949`).

The tooth inverse reaches only

\[
\boxed{Q(s_0)=0.1006739\ldots<0.101<\pi/3,}
\tag{170.15}
\]

so the same monotone inverse branch is valid.

---

## 170.3 Keep the shared simplex correlated

Use (170.11) to write

\[
w=s_0-x-y-z.
\]

Set

\[
r={10s_0\over3}-{7x\over3}-{10y\over3}-{10z\over3},
\]

\[
C=U_0-x-y+2z,
\]

\[
B=Q(y)+{10s_0\over3}-{7x\over3}-{7y\over3}-{10z\over3}.
\tag{170.16}
\]

Let `mathcal F(x,y,z)` denote the endpoint-relaxed lower bound for `widetilde J`.
The area derivative with respect to `r` is

\[
A_r=-2G'(\phi_8+r)-G'(r)<0.
\]

Direct differentiation gives

\[
\mathcal F_x
=-{7\over3}A_r
+{47\over6}\lambda_0
+2B+{14\over3}C.
\tag{170.17}
\]

Every term is positive, so every minimizer has

\[
\boxed{x=0.}
\tag{170.18}
\]

Similarly,

\[
\mathcal F_z
=-{10\over3}A_r
-\lambda_0\left(10+{1\over\kappa}-{25\over3}\right)
-4B+{20\over3}C.
\tag{170.19}
\]

Throughout the simplex,

\[
C\ge U_0-s_0=1.81930858\ldots,
\]

\[
B\le Q(s_0)+{10s_0\over3}<0.158008.
\]

Even after dropping the favorable area derivative,

\[
\boxed{\mathcal F_z>6.225.}
\tag{170.20}
\]

Therefore every minimizer also has

\[
\boxed{z=0.}
\tag{170.21}
\]

This is the structural gain over §167: the sparse `z` penalty cannot simultaneously
sit at its independent maximum while the directional product is extremal.

---

## 170.4 One-dimensional convex minimization

After `x=z=0`, the relaxed problem is one-dimensional; call the exact function `H(y)`
for `0<=y<=s_0`.

For the tooth inverse,

\[
Q'={1\over2F'(Q)},
\qquad
Q''=-{F''(Q)\over4F'(Q)^3}<0,
\]

where

\[
F(q)=\sin q+\sqrt3(1-\cos q)-q.
\]

Because `Q<0.101`,

\[
F'(Q)\le\sqrt3Q<0.175,
\qquad
F''(Q)>1.62,
\]

so

\[
-Q''>75.7,
\qquad
Q'>1/(2\cdot0.175)>2.857.
\tag{170.22}
\]

The area term has second derivative bounded below by `-25/6`, while
`U_0-y>1.8193`. Direct differentiation therefore gives the uniform bound

\[
\boxed{H''>273.}
\tag{170.23}
\]

Thus `H` is strictly convex and has at most one interior minimizer. Moreover

\[
H'(0.00669)=-0.0068266\ldots<0,
\]

\[
H'(0.00670)=0.0059257\ldots>0,
\]

so the unique minimizer lies in that interval.

At its midpoint,

\[
H(0.006695)=0.0023269894\ldots .
\]

Within the bracket, moving at most `5e-6` from the midpoint can lower `H` by less than
`3.5e-8`. Hence

\[
\boxed{\min H>0.0023269>0.00232.}
\tag{170.24}
\]

Therefore `widetilde J>0` uniformly on the entire relaxed simplex, contradicting the
necessary signed-return condition.

---

## 170.5 Consequence and frontier

There is no audited-working proof-only full-core Type-II survivor with

\[
\boxed{0\le\sigma\le0.0172.}
\tag{170.25}
\]

Thus every surviving full-core branch has

\[
\boxed{\sigma>0.0172.}
\tag{170.26}
\]

In the associated Section-101 parametrization this also forces

\[
\theta>2.706564629265182\ldots,
\qquad
\lambda=\sin(\theta/2)>0.976436949372909\ldots .
\tag{170.27}
\]

The same correlated scalar envelope first reaches zero near
`0.0173116294...`; further progress should change the inequality or add a new correlated
currency rather than merely push the endpoint decimals.

## Scope audit

- Endpoint relaxation has the safe inequality direction for the area term, directional
  product, and sparse penalty.
- The relaxed inequality simplex is larger than the physical equality simplex; filling
  unused slack with `w` is only a minimization device.
- The coarse `delta>=0.1` branch remains excluded and all tangent/gap prerequisites stay
  strictly inside their audited domains.
- The `x` and `z` eliminations are analytic, not sampled optimization.
- Strict convexity of `H` is uniform with large margin; the numerical minimum is not a
  sign-at-zero computation.
- No contact-isolated coordinate is inserted into a positive-core affine kernel.
- §§168–169 use independent train-atomic and outer-direction currencies and are
  compatible but not required premises.
- Type-I inactive containers are not addressed.

**Independent audit disposition:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. C-II and
Level I remain open.
